CREATED:20260913T201324Z
DESCRIPTION:Photographer and Duke MFA|EDA alumna Cassandra Klos explores h
 umanity's relationship to space\, scientific exploration\, and the possib
 ility of life beyond Earth in "Cosmic Origins\," an exhibition on view at
  CDS October 22 - January 4\, 2026.\n\nGALLERY HOURS\n\n**CDS will be clo
 sed to visitors on Friday\, October 30.**\n\nMonday - Thursday: 9 am - 5 
 pm.\nFriday: 9 am - 4 pm.\n\nBringing together photographs\, artifacts\, 
 and ephemera from simulated space missions\, the exhibition features work
  from Klos's long-term project "Mars on Earth\," which documents her expe
 riences as an analog astronaut participating in Earth-based simulations d
 esigned to replicate the conditions\, challenges\, and isolation of space
  exploration.\n\nWith pressurized spacesuits\, freeze-dried food\, roboti
 c companions\, and Mission Control waiting back at "home\," Klos's photog
 raphs visualize what life beyond Earth might look and feel like for the f
 irst cosmic explorers. The exhibition also includes spacesuits and materi
 als from Klos's fieldwork\, as well as work originally produced for Natio
 nal Geographic that will be exhibited together in the CDS Kreps Gallery f
 or the first time.\n\nBlurring the boundaries between documentary evidenc
 e and speculative imagination\, "Cosmic Origins" invites visitors to cons
 ider not only how humans might live on the Moon or Mars\, but also what s
 pace exploration can teach us about life on Earth.\n\nMore info about the
  exhibition: https://documentarystudies.duke.edu/upcoming-exhibitions. Or
 ganized by CDS in collaboration with Duke University's MFA|EDA program an
 d the Duke SPACE Initiative\, with support from Duke's Office of Interdis
 ciplinary Programs.
