CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0183a7-83184018-0184-87170e31-00006277:Hinz\, Nic
 olle
CREATED:20261012T185731Z
DESCRIPTION:Computational models of cardiac mechanics can serve as vital p
 redictive tools to assist our understanding of cardiac pathologies. For e
 xample\, we have developed a novel pipeline to convert pre-operative clin
 ical images of the mitral valve into predictive patient specific models c
 apable of accurately predicting the post-repair state. However\, the slow
  computational speed of traditional finite element models limits their pr
 actical clinical application. We have previously demonstrated the efficac
 y of the neural network finite element (NNFE) approach for predicting car
 diac mechanics of an idealized left ventricle within clinically relevant 
 timeframes without compromising accuracy. In this work\, we demonstrate t
 hat the NNFE approach can 1) predict the complex deformation patterns ari
 sing due to the presence of infarct tissue\, 2) predict the effects of in
 farct occurring in varying locations and of varying sizes\, and 3) captur
 e the compensatory mechanisms of the healthy tissue that displays a highe
 r contractile level in order to maintain stroke volume. These results rep
 resent an important first step towards high-speed patient specific infarc
 t modeling. While still in its early stages\, this approach paves the pat
 hway for high-speed patient-specific clinical simulations.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20261012T185731Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261012T185731Z
