CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=00f1fcdb-0f068baf-010f-068baf83-00000004:None
CREATED:20261019T173908Z
DESCRIPTION:Superionic conductors are crystalline solids exhibiting liquid
 -like ionic diffusivity\, making them promising for solid-state battery a
 pplications. Yet\, the atomistic mechanisms governing their transport pro
 perties remain poorly understood.  Here\, I combine neutron scattering ex
 periments with atomistic simulations to investigate the atomic structure 
 and dynamics underlying ionic diffusion in argyrodite-type superionic mat
 erials.\n\nI first investigate collective ionic hopping\, long proposed a
 s an origin of superionicity. Neutron scattering measurements on single-c
 rystal Cu7PS6 reveal short-range dynamical correlations among diffusing i
 ons\, providing direct experimental evidence of collective hopping and re
 solving its spatiotemporal characteristics. Machine-learned molecular dyn
 amics simulations reproduce these observations and provide complementary 
 insights into the hopping mechanism. By tracking the dynamical structure 
 factor across the superionic transition\, we further establish that colle
 ctive hopping emerges from the breakdown of low-energy\, Cu-dominated pho
 nons in the ordered phase. These results establish a direct connection be
 tween precursor phonons and collective hopping\, with similar behavior ob
 served across several Ag- and Cu-based argyrodites. Our analysis also rev
 eals that spectral weight transfer from phonons to collective hopping giv
 es rise to liquid-like dynamics\, marked by a crossover in the low-energy
  vibrational density of states from quadratic to linear scaling. Together
 \, these findings pinpoint strongly anharmonic phonons as a common origin
  of collective hopping and liquid-like dynamics.\n\nNext\, I investigate 
 the role of the host lattice in enabling fast ionic diffusion in Li argyr
 odites\, technologically important solid electrolytes. We show that frame
 work disorder and dynamics play distinct but cooperative roles. Static di
 sorder creates percolating pathways for Li-ion diffusion\, while host-lat
 tice fluctuations dynamically open local bottlenecks. This cooperative me
 chanism provides a microscopic design principle for high-performance soli
 d electrolytes.\n\nTaken together\, these studies establish the microscop
 ic origins of ionic transport in superionic argyrodites and highlight the
  role of dynamical correlations. These findings go beyond conventional de
 scriptions based on static potential energy surfaces for ionic hopping an
 d provide microscopic insights into superionic transport.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20261019T173908Z
