CREATED:20261013T172140Z
DESCRIPTION:In this talk\, Kelly Nguyen shares the story behind her book\,
  Critical Classicality in Vietnam and the Diaspora (Oxford University Pre
 ss\, 2026)\, and the poetry that emerged alongside it. The book traces ho
 w Vietnamese writers and thinkers have engaged with Greco-Roman antiquity
 \, developing the concept of critical classicality: a way of confronting 
 the classical tradition's complicity in colonialism and empire while also
  asking how those historically marginalized by that tradition might recla
 im and remake it for their own liberatory purposes.\nBut what happens whe
 n the histories we study refuse to stay on the page? Nguyen turns from sc
 holarship to poetry\, sharing select poems that were written alongside th
 e book as a way of metabolizing the violence\, silences\, and trauma of t
 he archives. Reimagining the Homeric Odyssey through the experiences of V
 ietnamese refugees\, including her own and those of her parents\, these p
 oems put critical classicality into practice. The event concludes with a 
 conversation with graduate students in Comparative Literature and Classic
 s.\n\nReception to follow in Allen 103A.
DURATION:PT2H
DTSTAMP:20261014T205909Z
